49764	49924	49456	49456	she	1542	“ I dunno . Some folk have a great wheel at one end o ’ their carding - rooms to make a draught , and carry off th ’ dust ; but that wheel costs a deal of money — five or six hundred pounds , maybe , and brings in no profit ; so it ’s but a few of th ’ masters as will put ’em up ; and I ’ve heard tell o ’ men who did n’t like working in places where there was a wheel , because they said as how it made ’em hungry , at after they ’d been long used to swallowing fluff , to go without it , and that their wages ought to be raised if they were to work in such places . So between masters and men th ’ wheels fall through . I know I wish there ’d been a wheel in our place , though . ”
